WEBVTT

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.535
Unauthorized-access hunts examine whether people or systems reached OT assets outside

00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:05.585
approved identity,

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:07.010
path, time, or privilege.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.920
Start with the authorized-access model: which vendors, employees, service accounts,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.755
jump hosts, VPNs, destinations, and maintenance windows are expected.

00:00:14.755 --> 00:00:20.075
Review remote-access and VPN sessions for unusual source systems, locations, login times,

00:00:20.075 --> 00:00:21.630
duration, and targets.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.910
Correlate jump-host activity with identity events, ticket approval, firewall connections,

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:26.840
and destination logs.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.895
A successful session without a corresponding work record may require validation.

00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:32.835
Shared accounts complicate attribution.

00:00:32.835 --> 00:00:34.750
Hunters should identify where they remain,

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.150
whether use can be tied to an individual session, and whether privilege exceeds the task.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.860
Service accounts deserve separate baselines because interactive or cross-zone use may be

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:43.795
unexpected.

00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:46.280
Look for access that bypasses managed entry points,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.940
originates from an unapproved workstation, reaches an unusual asset,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.235
or continues beyond the authorized window.

00:00:52.235 --> 00:00:56.535
Failed logons, privilege changes, and access after account expiration can add context,

00:00:56.535 --> 00:00:58.570
but none proves intent by itself.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.835
Coordinate findings with operations and the access owner.

00:01:01.835 --> 00:01:05.036
Emergency support, outages, or field work may explain unusual timing.

00:01:05.036 --> 00:01:07.790
Preserve session and approval evidence before it expires,

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.825
and avoid terminating active access without understanding process consequence.

00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:15.240
The hunt should conclude with affected accounts and assets, evidence, confidence,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.065
and recommended follow-up.

00:01:17.065 --> 00:01:21.365
Findings may improve access policy, account ownership, monitoring, vendor procedures,

00:01:21.365 --> 00:01:23.900
and detections for future unauthorized pathways.
